1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:00,788
Welcome.

2
00:00:00,838 --> 00:00:05,970
In this lecture we're going to secure Azure platform services, things like Azure SQL,

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:10,481
Storage and Key Vault, behind a Palo Alto Networks firewall that sits in a

4
00:00:10,531 --> 00:00:11,700
hub virtual network.

5
00:00:12,650 --> 00:00:15,147
PaaS services are different from virtual machines.

6
00:00:15,197 --> 00:00:17,337
They don't live in your network by default.

7
00:00:17,387 --> 00:00:21,464
They have public endpoints on the Microsoft backbone, and you have to choose how,

8
00:00:21,514 --> 00:00:24,520
or whether, your traffic to them goes through your firewall.

9
00:00:25,470 --> 00:00:27,568
So we'll walk through every option:

10
00:00:27,618 --> 00:00:34,074
public endpoints with IP rules, service endpoints, private endpoints, VNet injection, VNet integration for

11
00:00:34,124 --> 00:00:38,862
outbound traffic, publishing apps inbound, and the Network Security Perimeter.

12
00:00:38,912 --> 00:00:43,957
Then we'll compare the Palo Alto form factors, VM-Series and Cloud NGFW, and finish

13
00:00:44,007 --> 00:00:45,307
with a decision guide.

14
00:00:45,357 --> 00:00:49,358
Along the way we'll go deep on the part that trips most people up:

15
00:00:49,408 --> 00:00:53,900
the routing and DNS that actually force PaaS traffic through the firewall.

16
00:00:54,850 --> 00:01:00,620
Let's start with the reference topology we'll use for the whole lecture.

17
00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:04,986
In the middle is the hub virtual network, 10.0.0.0/16.

18
00:01:05,036 --> 00:01:07,330
Everything shared lives here:

19
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:10,240
the firewalls, the gateways and DNS.

20
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:15,017
Two Palo Alto VM-Series firewalls sit across three subnets.

21
00:01:15,067 --> 00:01:19,091
The management interface is in snet-mgmt, managed by Panorama.

22
00:01:19,141 --> 00:01:22,969
Ethernet 1/1 is in the untrust subnet, facing the internet.

23
00:01:23,019 --> 00:01:27,109
Ethernet 1/2 is in the trust subnet, facing everything private:

24
00:01:27,159 --> 00:01:30,460
the spokes, on-premises, and the private endpoints.

25
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:36,465
In Azure you don't run firewalls with a shared virtual MAC like on-premises.

26
00:01:36,515 --> 00:01:39,757
Instead you put Azure load balancers around them.

27
00:01:39,807 --> 00:01:45,534
A public load balancer distributes inbound internet traffic to the untrust interfaces.

28
00:01:45,584 --> 00:01:51,848
An internal Standard load balancer with an HA ports rule, frontend 10.0.2.100, distributes all

29
00:01:51,898 --> 00:01:54,535
private traffic to the trust interfaces.

30
00:01:54,585 --> 00:01:59,640
That frontend IP is the next hop that every route in this lecture points to.

31
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:07,943
On the left is on-premises, 172.16.0.0/16, connected with ExpressRoute or VPN to a gateway

32
00:02:07,993 --> 00:02:10,575
in the GatewaySubnet of the hub.

33
00:02:10,625 --> 00:02:17,648
Traffic from on-premises arrives at that gateway, and we'll steer it to the firewall's

34
00:02:17,698 --> 00:02:19,540
internal load balancer.

35
00:02:20,490 --> 00:02:21,937
At the bottom is DNS.

36
00:02:21,987 --> 00:02:27,996
An Azure DNS Private Resolver gives on-premises a target IP, 10.0.3.4, that can query

37
00:02:28,046 --> 00:02:29,707
Azure private DNS zones.

38
00:02:29,757 --> 00:02:35,553
And the private DNS zones, the privatelink zones, hold the private IP addresses of

39
00:02:35,603 --> 00:02:36,907
our PaaS resources.

40
00:02:36,957 --> 00:02:38,547
Keep these two in mind;

41
00:02:38,597 --> 00:02:40,400
DNS is half of this story.

42
00:02:41,350 --> 00:02:44,364
On the right, spoke one holds workloads:

43
00:02:44,414 --> 00:02:47,505
a VM at 10.1.1.10 and an App Service app.

44
00:02:47,555 --> 00:02:52,790
The spoke is peered to the hub, and its route table sends the default

45
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:56,620
route to the internal load balancer at 10.0.2.100.

46
00:02:57,570 --> 00:02:59,822
Spoke two holds private endpoints:

47
00:02:59,872 --> 00:03:04,425
SQL at 10.2.1.4, blob storage at 10.2.1.5 and Key Vault at 10.2.1.6.

48
00:03:04,475 --> 00:03:10,652
Each private endpoint connects over Private Link to the real PaaS resource on the far right.

49
00:03:10,702 --> 00:03:14,646
The goal of this lecture is simple to say and tricky to do:

50
00:03:14,696 --> 00:03:20,332
every flow to those PaaS resources, from spokes, from on-premises, and from the PaaS

51
00:03:20,382 --> 00:03:25,680
services themselves, should be inspected and logged by the Palo Alto firewalls.

52
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:28,226
Here's the map of options.

53
00:03:28,276 --> 00:03:32,024
The top row is about how your clients reach a PaaS resource.

54
00:03:32,074 --> 00:03:32,594
Option A:

55
00:03:32,644 --> 00:03:37,026
keep the public endpoint and lock it down to the firewall's egress IP.

56
00:03:37,076 --> 00:03:37,595
Option B:

57
00:03:37,645 --> 00:03:38,735
service endpoints.

58
00:03:38,785 --> 00:03:39,305
Option C:

59
00:03:39,355 --> 00:03:43,989
private endpoints, which is the main event and the default for production.

60
00:03:44,039 --> 00:03:44,559
Option D:

61
00:03:44,609 --> 00:03:49,940
VNet injection, where a dedicated version of the service runs inside your own subnet.

62
00:03:50,890 --> 00:03:55,329
The bottom row covers the other directions and the platform choice.

63
00:03:55,379 --> 00:03:55,931
Option E:

64
00:03:55,981 --> 00:04:01,693
outbound traffic from PaaS, like an App Service calling the internet or your database.

65
00:04:01,743 --> 00:04:02,296
Option F:

66
00:04:02,346 --> 00:04:06,315
inbound publishing, getting internet users to an app safely.

67
00:04:06,365 --> 00:04:06,918
Option G:

68
00:04:06,968 --> 00:04:12,412
the Network Security Perimeter, for the public side of PaaS that can't go private.

69
00:04:12,462 --> 00:04:12,814
And H:

70
00:04:12,864 --> 00:04:16,029
which Palo Alto form factor carries all of this.

71
00:04:16,079 --> 00:04:18,106
These options aren't exclusive;

72
00:04:18,156 --> 00:04:20,920
a real design usually combines C, E and F.

73
00:04:21,870 --> 00:04:22,396
Option A:

74
00:04:22,446 --> 00:04:23,676
the public endpoint.

75
00:04:23,726 --> 00:04:29,372
The App VM resolves the storage account's normal public name and connects to a public IP.

76
00:04:29,422 --> 00:04:35,004
Its default route sends the packet to the internal load balancer, the VM-Series inspects

77
00:04:35,054 --> 00:04:40,252
it, and then source-NATs it to the firewall's egress public IP, 20.55.10.7, out of

78
00:04:40,302 --> 00:04:41,660
the untrust interface.

79
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:48,083
The packet travels the Microsoft backbone to the storage account's public endpoint.

80
00:04:48,133 --> 00:04:53,938
On the storage account, under Networking, you set public access to selected networks and

81
00:04:53,988 --> 00:04:55,003
add one IP rule:

82
00:04:55,053 --> 00:04:56,134
allow 20.55.10.7.

83
00:04:56,184 --> 00:05:02,323
Because every request now carries the firewall's address, only traffic that went through your

84
00:05:02,373 --> 00:05:03,720
firewall is accepted.

85
00:05:04,670 --> 00:05:09,960
Anyone else on the internet hits the same endpoint and is refused by the IP rule.

86
00:05:10,010 --> 00:05:15,233
On the firewall side, you can use App-ID, URL filtering on the storage account's

87
00:05:15,283 --> 00:05:21,100
FQDN, and Threat Prevention, because the flow is plain HTTPS leaving through your egress.

88
00:05:22,050 --> 00:05:22,664
The upside:

89
00:05:22,714 --> 00:05:25,563
no DNS changes, and it works with every service.

90
00:05:25,613 --> 00:05:27,435
The downsides are real, though.

91
00:05:27,485 --> 00:05:29,549
The endpoint stays internet-facing.

92
00:05:29,599 --> 00:05:34,138
Every workload behind the firewall shares that one source IP, so the IP rule

93
00:05:34,188 --> 00:05:35,769
trusts all of them equally.

94
00:05:35,819 --> 00:05:38,245
There's no private path from on-premises.

95
00:05:38,295 --> 00:05:43,318
And unless the firewall restricts which storage accounts can be reached, data can be

96
00:05:43,368 --> 00:05:45,250
copied to anyone else's account.

97
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:49,840
Use it as a stepping stone, or for services that don't support Private Link.

98
00:05:50,790 --> 00:05:51,321
Option B:

99
00:05:51,371 --> 00:05:53,515
virtual network service endpoints.

100
00:05:53,565 --> 00:05:58,871
A service endpoint extends your subnet's identity to a service like Storage or SQL,

101
00:05:58,921 --> 00:06:00,355
so the service can say:

102
00:06:00,405 --> 00:06:02,743
only accept traffic from this subnet.

103
00:06:02,793 --> 00:06:08,164
Traffic still goes to the public endpoint, but over an optimized backbone route, and

104
00:06:08,214 --> 00:06:10,423
the source is your private address.

105
00:06:10,473 --> 00:06:14,940
The classic mistake is turning the endpoint on in the workload subnet.

106
00:06:15,890 --> 00:06:17,553
Look at the effective routes.

107
00:06:17,603 --> 00:06:22,517
The default route points at the firewall, but enabling the service endpoint adds the

108
00:06:22,567 --> 00:06:26,535
service's prefixes with a next hop of VirtualNetworkServiceEndpoint.

109
00:06:26,585 --> 00:06:30,611
Those prefixes are more specific than the default route, so they win.

110
00:06:30,661 --> 00:06:34,393
Storage traffic leaves directly and never touches the Palo Alto.

111
00:06:34,443 --> 00:06:36,520
You've just built a firewall bypass.

112
00:06:37,470 --> 00:06:38,251
The right way:

113
00:06:38,301 --> 00:06:42,881
leave service endpoints off in the spokes, and enable them on the subnet where

114
00:06:42,931 --> 00:06:44,543
traffic leaves the firewall.

115
00:06:44,593 --> 00:06:48,342
For a VM-Series that's the untrust subnet, the egress interface.

116
00:06:48,392 --> 00:06:53,387
The spoke sends traffic to the firewall with its default route, the firewall inspects

117
00:06:53,437 --> 00:06:58,373
it, source-NATs to its untrust interface, and forwards it out to the storage service

118
00:06:58,423 --> 00:06:59,620
through the endpoint.

119
00:07:00,570 --> 00:07:06,319
Now the storage firewall gets a virtual network rule that allows only the hub's untrust subnet.

120
00:07:06,369 --> 00:07:10,836
The Palo Alto does the per-workload control with App-ID and URL filtering.

121
00:07:10,886 --> 00:07:15,414
And add a service endpoint policy to that subnet, so traffic through it can

122
00:07:15,464 --> 00:07:17,672
only reach storage accounts you list.

123
00:07:17,722 --> 00:07:21,640
That's your guard against exfiltration to someone else's account.

124
00:07:22,590 --> 00:07:23,710
Limits to remember:

125
00:07:23,760 --> 00:07:26,544
it's still the public endpoint and public DNS.

126
00:07:26,594 --> 00:07:29,500
On-premises can't use a service endpoint at all.

127
00:07:29,550 --> 00:07:35,044
The service trusts the whole firewall subnet, so all control lives on the firewall policy.

128
00:07:35,094 --> 00:07:37,569
And only a subset of services support it.

129
00:07:37,619 --> 00:07:42,681
Service endpoints are a fine, cheap option for Azure-only access to a few services,

130
00:07:42,731 --> 00:07:46,500
but for most production work, we move on to private endpoints.

131
00:07:47,450 --> 00:07:47,964
Option C:

132
00:07:48,014 --> 00:07:49,091
private endpoints.

133
00:07:49,141 --> 00:07:53,787
A private endpoint is a network interface in your subnet with a private IP,

134
00:07:53,837 --> 00:07:58,295
here 10.2.1.5, that maps to one specific resource, and one sub-resource:

135
00:07:58,345 --> 00:08:00,549
this storage account's blob service.

136
00:08:00,599 --> 00:08:06,498
Behind the scenes, Private Link carries the traffic to the service over the Microsoft backbone.

137
00:08:06,548 --> 00:08:11,194
And because the endpoint maps to one resource, not to the whole service, it

138
00:08:11,244 --> 00:08:15,640
gives you built-in protection against data going to some other account.

139
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:21,693
On the resource, the endpoint connection must be approved, and then you set public

140
00:08:21,743 --> 00:08:23,390
network access to disabled.

141
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:24,961
That second step matters:

142
00:08:25,011 --> 00:08:29,548
creating a private endpoint does not close the public endpoint by itself.

143
00:08:29,598 --> 00:08:30,428
You need both.

144
00:08:30,478 --> 00:08:35,769
Storage needs one endpoint per sub-resource, so blob, dfs, file, queue, table and web

145
00:08:35,819 --> 00:08:38,220
are separate endpoints if you use them.

146
00:08:39,170 --> 00:08:43,236
Now DNS, because applications keep using the normal name.

147
00:08:43,286 --> 00:08:47,786
The app looks up stgdemo dot blob dot core dot windows dot net.

148
00:08:47,836 --> 00:08:51,831
Public DNS answers with a CNAME to the privatelink name:

149
00:08:51,881 --> 00:08:56,308
stgdemo dot privatelink dot blob dot core dot windows dot net.

150
00:08:56,358 --> 00:09:02,880
That redirection happens for everyone in the world, the moment the private endpoint exists.

151
00:09:03,830 --> 00:09:09,015
If the asking VNet is linked to the private DNS zone privatelink dot blob

152
00:09:09,065 --> 00:09:13,605
dot core dot windows dot net, the zone answers with an A record:

153
00:09:13,655 --> 00:09:14,250
10.2.1.5.

154
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:19,700
The client connects to a private IP, and nothing in the application changes.

155
00:09:20,650 --> 00:09:25,080
Anyone not linked to the zone follows the CNAME to the public IP, and

156
00:09:25,130 --> 00:09:27,677
is refused because public access is off.

157
00:09:27,727 --> 00:09:32,352
So when a private endpoint "doesn't work", the first question is always:

158
00:09:32,402 --> 00:09:34,560
did the client get the private IP?

159
00:09:35,510 --> 00:09:39,739
In a hub-and-spoke design we centralize that DNS.

160
00:09:39,789 --> 00:09:43,582
The privatelink zones are linked to the hub.

161
00:09:43,632 --> 00:09:52,227
The DNS Private Resolver's inbound endpoint, 10.0.3.4, sends queries to Azure DNS at 168.63.129.16,

162
00:09:52,277 --> 00:09:57,380
which consults the linked zones and returns the private IP.

163
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:01,882
On-premises, the client asks its normal DNS server.

164
00:10:01,932 --> 00:10:07,391
That server has a conditional forwarder for the public zone, blob dot core dot

165
00:10:07,441 --> 00:10:11,982
windows dot net, pointing at 10.0.3.4 across ExpressRoute or VPN.

166
00:10:12,032 --> 00:10:17,915
Forward the public zone name, not just the privatelink zone, because the client asks

167
00:10:17,965 --> 00:10:19,327
for the public name.

168
00:10:19,377 --> 00:10:27,520
On-premises servers can't reach 168.63.129.16 directly, which is exactly why the resolver's inbound endpoint exists.

169
00:10:28,470 --> 00:10:30,025
The firewall needs DNS too.

170
00:10:30,075 --> 00:10:34,425
If you build FQDN address objects on the Palo Alto, set the firewall's DNS

171
00:10:34,475 --> 00:10:38,170
server to the resolver, so the FQDN resolves to the private IP.

172
00:10:38,220 --> 00:10:43,580
Otherwise the firewall resolves the public IP and your rule never matches the real traffic.

173
00:10:44,530 --> 00:10:46,905
For the spokes you have three choices.

174
00:10:46,955 --> 00:10:50,734
Point their custom DNS at the resolver for one central path.

175
00:10:50,784 --> 00:10:56,222
Or keep Azure-provided DNS and link the zones, or a forwarding ruleset, to each spoke.

176
00:10:56,272 --> 00:11:01,263
Or point spokes at the firewall's DNS proxy, which forwards to the resolver, so

177
00:11:01,313 --> 00:11:03,560
every lookup is logged and filtered.

178
00:11:04,510 --> 00:11:05,773
The common gotchas:

179
00:11:05,823 --> 00:11:13,720
forwarding only privatelink zones on-premises, pointing on-premises at 168.63.129.16, and letting every team create

180
00:11:13,770 --> 00:11:16,346
its own copy of the privatelink zones.

181
00:11:16,396 --> 00:11:22,220
Keep one set per environment and let Azure Policy register the records automatically.

182
00:11:23,170 --> 00:11:25,196
Now the heart of the lecture:

183
00:11:25,246 --> 00:11:29,133
forcing private endpoint traffic through the Palo Alto.

184
00:11:29,183 --> 00:11:35,002
The App VM in spoke one wants to reach the private endpoint 10.2.1.5 in spoke two.

185
00:11:35,052 --> 00:11:38,295
Spoke one has a default route to the firewall.

186
00:11:38,345 --> 00:11:40,156
You'd think that's enough.

187
00:11:40,206 --> 00:11:40,800
It isn't.

188
00:11:41,750 --> 00:11:45,141
Look at the effective routes on the app subnet.

189
00:11:45,191 --> 00:11:48,070
There's our default route to 10.0.2.100.

190
00:11:48,120 --> 00:11:51,877
There's the peering route to spoke two, 10.2.0.0/16.

191
00:11:51,927 --> 00:11:58,174
And there's a slash 32 route for 10.2.1.5 with next hop InterfaceEndpoint, injected by

192
00:11:58,224 --> 00:12:02,201
the private endpoint and propagated across the peering.

193
00:12:02,251 --> 00:12:06,155
Longest prefix wins, so the slash 32 beats everything.

194
00:12:06,205 --> 00:12:11,500
The traffic goes straight to the endpoint and the firewall never sees it.

195
00:12:12,450 --> 00:12:13,764
The fix has two parts.

196
00:12:13,814 --> 00:12:14,322
Part one:

197
00:12:14,372 --> 00:12:19,841
on the subnet that hosts the private endpoints, enable private endpoint network policies.

198
00:12:19,891 --> 00:12:26,725
The property is privateEndpointNetworkPolicies, set to RouteTableEnabled, or Enabled, which turns on both route

199
00:12:26,775 --> 00:12:28,895
tables and network security groups.

200
00:12:28,945 --> 00:12:34,600
Network policies are off by default, and this setting affects every endpoint in that subnet.

201
00:12:35,550 --> 00:12:36,104
Part two:

202
00:12:36,154 --> 00:12:42,415
on the client subnet, add a user-defined route for the private endpoint range to the firewall.

203
00:12:42,465 --> 00:12:46,577
The rule is that the route must be at least as specific as the

204
00:12:46,627 --> 00:12:49,934
address space of the VNet that holds the endpoint.

205
00:12:49,984 --> 00:12:52,485
Here that's 10.2.0.0/16 to 10.0.2.100.

206
00:12:52,535 --> 00:12:56,514
With network policies on, that route overrides the slash 32.

207
00:12:56,564 --> 00:12:59,132
A default route alone will never do it.

208
00:12:59,182 --> 00:13:04,100
Now the flow goes VM, internal load balancer, VM-Series, private endpoint.

209
00:13:05,050 --> 00:13:07,754
Getting there is half the job.

210
00:13:07,804 --> 00:13:09,590
Now the return path.

211
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:16,200
The request leaves FW1 with the original source, 10.1.1.10, to 10.2.1.4.

212
00:13:17,150 --> 00:13:22,685
The private endpoint's data plane doesn't follow your route tables for its replies the

213
00:13:22,735 --> 00:13:23,659
way a VM would.

214
00:13:23,709 --> 00:13:28,855
The reply can head straight back to 10.1.1.10, skipping the firewall, or land on

215
00:13:28,905 --> 00:13:30,089
the other firewall.

216
00:13:30,139 --> 00:13:34,700
Either way the firewall sees half a session and drops it as asymmetric.

217
00:13:35,650 --> 00:13:37,737
Option one, the classic answer:

218
00:13:37,787 --> 00:13:39,598
source NAT on the firewall.

219
00:13:39,648 --> 00:13:43,527
FW1 rewrites the source to its trust interface, 10.0.2.4.

220
00:13:43,577 --> 00:13:49,868
The endpoint replies to 10.0.2.4, which can only be FW1, so the session is always symmetric.

221
00:13:49,918 --> 00:13:53,935
The cost is that the PaaS logs show the firewall's address;

222
00:13:53,985 --> 00:13:58,760
you correlate with the firewall's traffic log to find the real client.

223
00:13:59,710 --> 00:14:00,376
Option two:

224
00:14:00,426 --> 00:14:03,629
Microsoft now lets you drop that SNAT requirement.

225
00:14:03,679 --> 00:14:08,963
Add a tag named disableSnatOnPL, value true, to each firewall dataplane NIC, or to

226
00:14:09,013 --> 00:14:10,525
each scale set instance.

227
00:14:10,575 --> 00:14:15,990
The tag is case-sensitive, and it causes a one-time reset of private endpoint flows,

228
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:18,136
so do it in a maintenance window.

229
00:14:18,186 --> 00:14:23,536
Either way, keep both directions on the same internal load balancer frontend, so HA

230
00:14:23,586 --> 00:14:27,700
ports hashing sends the flow and its reply to the same firewall.

231
00:14:28,650 --> 00:14:31,354
Where should the private endpoints live?

232
00:14:31,404 --> 00:14:33,833
Pattern one, which we've been using:

233
00:14:33,883 --> 00:14:36,243
a dedicated private endpoint spoke.

234
00:14:36,293 --> 00:14:40,926
Application subnets get one route, 10.2.0.0/16 to the load balancer.

235
00:14:40,976 --> 00:14:46,228
DNS zone groups, NSGs and firewall address objects all live in one place, and

236
00:14:46,278 --> 00:14:48,844
the security zone boundary is obvious.

237
00:14:48,894 --> 00:14:49,877
It scales well;

238
00:14:49,927 --> 00:14:53,320
you just need clear ownership of that shared VNet.

239
00:14:54,270 --> 00:14:55,030
Pattern two:

240
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:57,258
endpoints in a subnet in the hub.

241
00:14:57,308 --> 00:15:02,795
Spokes route 10.0.4.0/24 to the load balancer, which is more specific than the hub

242
00:15:02,845 --> 00:15:08,128
space, so it works, and the GatewaySubnet needs the same route for on-premises.

243
00:15:08,178 --> 00:15:14,340
Fewer VNets, but the hub grows, and anything else in the hub reaches the endpoints directly.

244
00:15:15,290 --> 00:15:16,190
Pattern three:

245
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:19,992
the endpoint inside the workload spoke, next to the app.

246
00:15:20,042 --> 00:15:22,843
Now it's subnet-to-subnet inside one VNet.

247
00:15:22,893 --> 00:15:27,867
You add a route on the app subnet for the endpoint subnet, 10.1.5.0/24, to

248
00:15:27,917 --> 00:15:34,791
the load balancer, plus network policies on the endpoint subnet, and traffic hairpins through the hub.

249
00:15:34,841 --> 00:15:39,747
It gives app teams ownership, but it's the easiest to forget, and DNS and

250
00:15:39,797 --> 00:15:41,648
policy sprawl across spokes.

251
00:15:41,698 --> 00:15:46,400
Most enterprises use pattern one, with pattern three as the exception.

252
00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:48,513
Now on-premises.

253
00:15:48,563 --> 00:15:52,380
A client at 172.16.5.20 connects over ExpressRoute.

254
00:15:52,430 --> 00:15:58,825
The gateway learns the spoke prefixes through peering, so by default it sends traffic

255
00:15:58,875 --> 00:16:02,540
straight to the endpoint, bypassing the firewall.

256
00:16:03,490 --> 00:16:07,319
Associate a route table with the GatewaySubnet:

257
00:16:07,369 --> 00:16:09,464
10.2.0.0/16 to 10.0.2.100.

258
00:16:09,514 --> 00:16:15,818
With network policies on the endpoint subnet, the slash 32 doesn't bypass it.

259
00:16:15,868 --> 00:16:21,760
Now on-premises traffic goes gateway, load balancer, firewall, endpoint.

260
00:16:22,710 --> 00:16:29,116
For the return, the firewall routes 172.16.0.0/16 out of its trust interface toward the

261
00:16:29,166 --> 00:16:34,978
gateway, and SNAT or the disableSnatOnPL tag keeps the endpoint side symmetric.

262
00:16:35,028 --> 00:16:38,021
Two rules for GatewaySubnet route tables:

263
00:16:38,071 --> 00:16:44,180
never put a default route there, and don't disable gateway route propagation on it.

264
00:16:45,130 --> 00:16:48,183
Let's configure the Palo Alto side.

265
00:16:48,233 --> 00:16:49,158
Interfaces:

266
00:16:49,208 --> 00:16:57,580
ethernet 1/1 untrust at 10.0.1.4 in zone UNTRUST, ethernet 1/2 trust at 10.0.2.4 in zone TRUST.

267
00:16:57,630 --> 00:16:58,821
Static routes:

268
00:16:58,871 --> 00:17:05,913
the default route out of ethernet 1/1 to the untrust subnet's gateway, 10.0.1.1.

269
00:17:05,963 --> 00:17:13,006
All private ranges, the spokes and on-premises, out of ethernet 1/2 to 10.0.2.1.

270
00:17:13,056 --> 00:17:19,920
And a route for 168.63.129.16 out of each interface to its own subnet gateway,

271
00:17:19,970 --> 00:17:28,697
because that's the load balancer health probe source, plus an interface management profile allowing

272
00:17:28,747 --> 00:17:35,080
HTTPS only from that address, so the probes see the firewall as healthy.

273
00:17:36,030 --> 00:17:36,455
Objects:

274
00:17:36,505 --> 00:17:41,502
static address objects for each endpoint, or FQDN objects that resolve to private IPs

275
00:17:41,552 --> 00:17:43,818
because the firewall uses the resolver.

276
00:17:43,868 --> 00:17:49,400
With Panorama, the Azure plugin pulls VNet, subnet and tag information from your subscriptions

277
00:17:49,450 --> 00:17:53,556
into Dynamic Address Groups, so rules follow workloads as they change.

278
00:17:53,606 --> 00:17:58,010
And a custom URL category listing your storage and Key Vault names lets you

279
00:17:58,060 --> 00:18:00,860
match on the TLS server name without decrypting.

280
00:18:01,810 --> 00:18:03,041
The security policy.

281
00:18:03,091 --> 00:18:07,844
Rule one allows the app subnet to the SQL endpoint with App-ID mssql-db and

282
00:18:07,894 --> 00:18:09,189
full threat profiles.

283
00:18:09,239 --> 00:18:10,086
A note on SQL:

284
00:18:10,136 --> 00:18:13,160
the Proxy connection policy uses only port 1433;

285
00:18:13,210 --> 00:18:18,347
Redirect through a private endpoint needs a much wider port range, so either open

286
00:18:18,397 --> 00:18:21,550
that range to the SQL endpoint only, or set Proxy.

287
00:18:21,600 --> 00:18:25,841
Rule two allows the app to blob over SSL, limited to your accounts.

288
00:18:25,891 --> 00:18:28,595
Rule three allows on-premises to Key Vault.

289
00:18:28,645 --> 00:18:32,693
Rule four denies and logs anything else to the endpoint subnets.

290
00:18:32,743 --> 00:18:34,487
Then two things people miss.

291
00:18:34,537 --> 00:18:39,930
Spoke-to-spoke traffic enters and leaves the same trust interface, so it's intrazone;

292
00:18:39,980 --> 00:18:43,261
override the intrazone default rule to deny and log.

293
00:18:43,311 --> 00:18:50,241
And a NAT rule source-translates traffic to the endpoint subnets, unless you've used the disableSnatOnPL tag.

294
00:18:50,291 --> 00:18:55,301
TLS decryption is possible for HTTPS where clients trust your CA, but SQL wraps

295
00:18:55,351 --> 00:18:59,720
TLS inside its own protocol, so control it with App-ID and addresses.

296
00:19:00,670 --> 00:19:01,190
Option D:

297
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:02,141
VNet injection.

298
00:19:02,191 --> 00:19:06,578
Some services have a dedicated tier that deploys into your own subnet:

299
00:19:06,628 --> 00:19:13,297
SQL Managed Instance, App Service Environment version 3, API Management in injected mode, AKS, Databricks.

300
00:19:13,347 --> 00:19:16,783
Once they're in your subnet, they behave much like VMs.

301
00:19:16,833 --> 00:19:21,728
A route table sends their traffic to the firewall, and other spokes reach them

302
00:19:21,778 --> 00:19:24,580
through the firewall like any other workload.

303
00:19:25,530 --> 00:19:27,560
The catch is the management plane.

304
00:19:27,610 --> 00:19:33,128
Each service has documented routes and NSG rules it needs to talk to Azure's control plane.

305
00:19:33,178 --> 00:19:39,307
SQL Managed Instance even uses service-aided subnet configuration that adds its own routes and rules;

306
00:19:39,357 --> 00:19:40,347
don't fight them.

307
00:19:40,397 --> 00:19:46,526
On the firewall, allow each service's documented dependencies, like Entra ID, monitoring and storage,

308
00:19:46,576 --> 00:19:50,870
and for AKS the container registries, and deny and log everything else.

309
00:19:50,920 --> 00:19:55,520
Injection gives the strongest isolation, at the highest cost and complexity.

310
00:19:56,470 --> 00:19:58,479
Option E covers the other direction:

311
00:19:58,529 --> 00:20:00,652
traffic that starts in a PaaS service.

312
00:20:00,702 --> 00:20:05,399
Multi-tenant App Service and Functions don't live in your VNet, so by default their

313
00:20:05,449 --> 00:20:07,859
outbound calls go straight to the internet.

314
00:20:07,909 --> 00:20:09,460
VNet integration fixes that.

315
00:20:10,410 --> 00:20:15,673
You give the app a delegated integration subnet, delegated to Microsoft dot Web slash

316
00:20:15,723 --> 00:20:21,860
serverFarms, and turn on route all, which in the current API is outboundVnetRouting dot allTraffic.

317
00:20:21,910 --> 00:20:28,423
Now all outbound traffic, including configuration traffic like image pulls, content share access, backups

318
00:20:28,473 --> 00:20:31,860
and managed identity token requests, enters the subnet.

319
00:20:31,910 --> 00:20:35,360
A default route on that subnet sends it to the firewall.

320
00:20:36,310 --> 00:20:41,923
The VM-Series applies App-ID and URL filtering to internet calls and source-NATs them to

321
00:20:41,973 --> 00:20:46,042
its public IP, and calls to SQL or Key Vault go to their private

322
00:20:46,092 --> 00:20:49,260
endpoints, through the same routing we just built.

323
00:20:50,210 --> 00:20:54,791
Remember to allow 443, and 445 if the app uses a content share, from

324
00:20:54,841 --> 00:20:56,358
the integration subnet.

325
00:20:56,408 --> 00:20:58,945
And VNet integration is outbound only:

326
00:20:58,995 --> 00:21:05,007
users reaching the app still need a private endpoint or one of the inbound patterns next.

327
00:21:05,057 --> 00:21:11,000
Functions Premium and Flex, Logic Apps Standard and Container Apps follow the same idea.

328
00:21:11,950 --> 00:21:12,414
Option F:

329
00:21:12,464 --> 00:21:14,358
publishing an app to the internet.

330
00:21:14,408 --> 00:21:15,044
Pattern one:

331
00:21:15,094 --> 00:21:18,016
Azure Front Door Premium with a Private Link origin.

332
00:21:18,066 --> 00:21:22,417
Front Door's WAF and edge absorb the internet, and it reaches the App Service

333
00:21:22,467 --> 00:21:26,418
over a Microsoft-managed private endpoint that you approve on the app.

334
00:21:26,468 --> 00:21:28,076
The app has no public origin.

335
00:21:28,126 --> 00:21:30,534
But notice the Palo Alto isn't in the path;

336
00:21:30,584 --> 00:21:32,420
inspection is the WAF's job here.

337
00:21:33,370 --> 00:21:34,056
Pattern two:

338
00:21:34,106 --> 00:21:39,082
Application Gateway with WAF first, then the VM-Series, then the private endpoint.

339
00:21:39,132 --> 00:21:43,189
The WAF sees the real client IP and adds an X-Forwarded-For header.

340
00:21:43,239 --> 00:21:48,215
A route on the Application Gateway subnet sends the backend prefix to the internal

341
00:21:48,265 --> 00:21:53,241
load balancer, and the firewall adds Threat Prevention and App-ID on the back leg,

342
00:21:53,291 --> 00:21:54,958
SNATing toward the endpoint.

343
00:21:55,008 --> 00:21:59,800
This is the most common combination when you want both a WAF and the Palo Alto.

344
00:22:00,750 --> 00:22:01,545
Pattern three:

345
00:22:01,595 --> 00:22:02,692
the firewall first.

346
00:22:02,742 --> 00:22:08,063
The public load balancer delivers traffic to the VM-Series, which does destination NAT to

347
00:22:08,113 --> 00:22:12,348
a private Application Gateway or endpoint, and source NAT for symmetry.

348
00:22:12,398 --> 00:22:17,056
You get one internet edge with Threat Prevention first, but the client IP only

349
00:22:17,106 --> 00:22:21,823
survives in the firewall logs, and seeing layer seven needs inbound decryption.

350
00:22:21,873 --> 00:22:26,531
A variant chains a Gateway Load Balancer to a public frontend, so the firewall

351
00:22:26,581 --> 00:22:28,100
is inserted transparently.

352
00:22:29,050 --> 00:22:29,587
Option G:

353
00:22:29,637 --> 00:22:31,610
the Network Security Perimeter.

354
00:22:31,660 --> 00:22:37,090
Some PaaS traffic never enters your VNets, like Key Vault sending diagnostics to Log

355
00:22:37,140 --> 00:22:40,221
Analytics, or services that have to stay public.

356
00:22:40,271 --> 00:22:45,310
A perimeter draws a boundary around those PaaS resources, and they can talk to

357
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:46,680
each other inside it.

358
00:22:47,630 --> 00:22:53,800
Inbound access rules allow IP prefixes or subscriptions, for example the firewall's egress IP.

359
00:22:53,850 --> 00:22:55,587
Outbound rules allow FQDNs.

360
00:22:55,637 --> 00:22:57,175
Everyone else is denied.

361
00:22:57,225 --> 00:23:02,733
Start in transition mode, which logs what would be blocked, then switch to enforced.

362
00:23:02,783 --> 00:23:05,380
Every allowed and denied call is logged.

363
00:23:06,330 --> 00:23:09,322
Private endpoint traffic isn't governed by the perimeter;

364
00:23:09,372 --> 00:23:10,764
that's your firewall's job.

365
00:23:10,814 --> 00:23:13,059
So the perimeter complements the Palo Alto:

366
00:23:13,109 --> 00:23:17,756
it covers the public side and PaaS-to-PaaS calls, and the firewall covers your networks.

367
00:23:17,806 --> 00:23:22,613
Service coverage differs, some are generally available and some in preview, so check before

368
00:23:22,663 --> 00:23:23,360
relying on it.

369
00:23:24,310 --> 00:23:24,899
Option H:

370
00:23:24,949 --> 00:23:28,092
which Palo Alto form factor carries all this?

371
00:23:28,142 --> 00:23:31,569
The VM-Series in the hub VNet is what we've used.

372
00:23:31,619 --> 00:23:32,705
You run the VMs:

373
00:23:32,755 --> 00:23:37,034
sizing, zones, upgrades, bootstrap and scale-set autoscaling.

374
00:23:37,084 --> 00:23:44,201
You get full PAN-OS, GlobalProtect, decryption and every profile, and Panorama with the Azure plugin.

375
00:23:44,251 --> 00:23:50,020
Run them active-active behind load balancers, or active-passive with floating IPs.

376
00:23:50,970 --> 00:23:54,626
Cloud NGFW for Azure in the hub VNet is the managed service.

377
00:23:54,676 --> 00:23:59,259
You delegate a public and a private subnet, Palo Alto runs scaling and high

378
00:23:59,309 --> 00:24:03,521
availability, and spokes send traffic with a route to its private IP.

379
00:24:03,571 --> 00:24:07,906
Policy comes from a local rulestack, Panorama, or Strata Cloud Manager.

380
00:24:07,956 --> 00:24:13,280
There are fewer knobs than full PAN-OS, so check the feature matrix against your needs.

381
00:24:14,230 --> 00:24:18,749
And if you run Azure Virtual WAN, Cloud NGFW deploys inside the virtual hub.

382
00:24:18,799 --> 00:24:24,160
Routing intent sends private and internet traffic to it, with no route tables to maintain.

383
00:24:24,210 --> 00:24:29,570
For private endpoints in spokes connected to that hub, enable route table network policies

384
00:24:29,620 --> 00:24:30,592
on their subnets.

385
00:24:30,642 --> 00:24:34,620
Without it, on-premises traffic to the endpoints can be asymmetric.

386
00:24:35,570 --> 00:24:37,691
The firewall is one layer of several.

387
00:24:37,741 --> 00:24:38,571
Identity first:

388
00:24:38,621 --> 00:24:44,438
Entra ID and managed identities, and turn off shared keys and local authentication wherever you can.

389
00:24:44,488 --> 00:24:45,964
Then the resource network:

390
00:24:46,014 --> 00:24:48,722
public access disabled, private endpoints only.

391
00:24:48,772 --> 00:24:49,660
Then the subnet:

392
00:24:49,710 --> 00:24:54,000
with network policies on, an NSG on the endpoint subnet, using application security groups,

393
00:24:55,050 --> 00:24:57,640
can allow only the firewall's SNAT addresses.

394
00:24:58,590 --> 00:25:04,671
The Palo Alto adds App-ID, Threat Prevention, URL and FQDN control, and a deny-and-log default.

395
00:25:04,721 --> 00:25:06,929
Governance keeps it true over time:

396
00:25:06,979 --> 00:25:12,415
Azure Policy can deny public network access, deploy private DNS zone groups for every

397
00:25:12,465 --> 00:25:14,931
new endpoint, and audit what's missing.

398
00:25:14,981 --> 00:25:21,062
And send firewall logs to Strata Logging Service or Microsoft Sentinel, alongside Azure Monitor

399
00:25:21,112 --> 00:25:22,740
and perimeter access logs.

400
00:25:23,690 --> 00:25:26,251
When it breaks, troubleshoot in this order.

401
00:25:26,301 --> 00:25:26,797
One, DNS:

402
00:25:26,847 --> 00:25:28,922
from the client, nslookup the name.

403
00:25:28,972 --> 00:25:32,200
You must see the privatelink CNAME and the private IP.

404
00:25:32,250 --> 00:25:32,929
Two, routes:

405
00:25:32,979 --> 00:25:35,539
check effective routes on the client's NIC.

406
00:25:35,589 --> 00:25:42,035
The endpoint prefix should point at the virtual appliance, with no active slash 32 InterfaceEndpoint route.

407
00:25:42,085 --> 00:25:47,560
The endpoint's own NIC doesn't show effective routes, so always check from the client side.

408
00:25:48,510 --> 00:25:53,176
Three, the firewall traffic log, filtered on the endpoint subnet:

409
00:25:53,226 --> 00:25:56,876
check the rule, the NAT and the session end reason.

410
00:25:56,926 --> 00:26:00,141
No log at all means the packet never arrived.

411
00:26:00,191 --> 00:26:01,520
Four, the endpoint:

412
00:26:01,570 --> 00:26:06,817
connection approved, and any NSG on its subnet allowing the SNAT address.

413
00:26:06,867 --> 00:26:07,905
Five, symmetry:

414
00:26:07,955 --> 00:26:14,580
if it works with SNAT but not without, check the disableSnatOnPL tag on every dataplane NIC.

415
00:26:15,530 --> 00:26:17,797
To wrap up, here's the decision guide.

416
00:26:17,847 --> 00:26:22,797
Public endpoints with IP rules and service endpoints are quick and cheap, but stay

417
00:26:22,847 --> 00:26:24,931
public and can't reach on-premises.

418
00:26:24,981 --> 00:26:29,016
Private endpoints with route tables are the default for production:

419
00:26:29,066 --> 00:26:33,955
the traffic goes through the Palo Alto, on-premises gets a private path, and each

420
00:26:34,005 --> 00:26:35,967
endpoint is tied to one resource.

421
00:26:36,017 --> 00:26:38,284
VNet injection is for dedicated tiers.

422
00:26:38,334 --> 00:26:41,394
VNet integration covers outbound traffic from PaaS.

423
00:26:41,444 --> 00:26:45,174
Front Door, Application Gateway or firewall DNAT publish apps.

424
00:26:45,224 --> 00:26:48,040
And the perimeter covers what can't go private.

425
00:26:48,990 --> 00:26:50,097
The rule of thumb:

426
00:26:50,147 --> 00:26:55,753
private endpoints in a dedicated spoke, network policies on, a route per endpoint prefix

427
00:26:55,803 --> 00:27:01,408
to the internal load balancer, SNAT or the disableSnatOnPL tag, the DNS Private Resolver

428
00:27:01,458 --> 00:27:06,357
for name resolution, public access off, and Azure Policy to keep it that way.

429
00:27:06,407 --> 00:27:10,535
The diagrams and the full script are in the demo-labs repository.

430
00:27:10,585 --> 00:27:11,820
Thanks for watching.
